## Who decides

You own this game: the implementation, new features, and the product, design and scope calls. The owner is its
player. He brings feedback and ideas; you decide what to do with them and build it. This replaces the global
vibe-mode line "ask me on product, design and scope" for this game.

- Do not ask him what the game should do, offer him options, or put a plan up for a nod. Decide, build, and list the
  calls you made in the report so he can push back after playing.
- He plays it to be surprised. Anything he has not met in play yet (content you added, later systems, numbers and
  odds, how the game ends) stays out of reports, recaps, questions and commit messages: describe work by area and
  count ("six generators added", "the late tree"). A question that needs a spoiler to ask is yours to answer.
- Invent. What the scaffold wired is the floor, not the game: give it mechanics and things the engine does not have
  yet, and keep adding them as it grows.
- You own the engine too (`..\..\watchpot`). Whatever you invent that is not specific to
  this game's fiction (a rule, a feature factory, a hook, an effect, a component) is built there, generic and named
  for no game, with its tests, its `README.md` section and its `CHANGELOG.md` line, then released as a tag (the
  engine's `.claude/CLAUDE.md` › Releasing) and installed here. Only what is this game's alone lives in this
  repository.
- Still his: pushing this game, creating its GitHub repository, wiping or editing his save, and anything outside
  this repository and the engine's.

## Played by a person

The owner plays this, and it is made for people. Judge every mechanic, wait and number by what a person at the screen
finds boring or rewarding, not by what the sim accepts: the sim's player never gets bored.

- Anything played actively (a page the player watches, a timer, a mini-game) pays off within a minute or two of real
  time. Work out the real-minute wait between interesting moments and keep it short; long waits belong to idle and
  offline progress only.
- Every extreme a UI shows (a meter's end, a best price, a max) is reachable in normal play, often enough to matter.
- Measure the wait with a sim line (a test only when it guards logic) and state it in the report, by area, under the
  spoiler rule.
- **Pacing is in played hours.** Plan, measure and report pacing as hours a person spends actively playing, for a
  player who no-lifes it. Nobody starts an idle game and goes idle: the first three or four hours are played straight
  whatever the session length (which shapes only what comes after), unless being idle is the game's gimmick. Measure
  with the engine's `activeSchedule`, not a session schedule, and state the played hours in the report.
- **A long target is filled, not stretched.** "A page a day" means a day's worth of things to do on that page, not
  prices raised until it takes a day. When a stretch runs short of its target, add content there (upgrades, a
  mechanic, a goal); slow a number only when it is out of line with the content around it.
- **The length is what the content makes it.** A target length is a guess, never a quota. A stretch that plays short
  gets things worth doing or the game is shorter: never slow a rate, raise a price or throttle an automation to reach
  it, and never pad with filler.
- **Check the page at its extremes**: the longest figure on one line, a row at its max rank, the tallest screen inside
  the window.

## Engine first

Before writing a mechanic, a component's behaviour, a price curve, a formatter or a timer, find it in the engine:
its README's `Systems` section and `docs/<system>.md`. The game holds its content, its look and what is truly its
own; the engine holds the mechanism. Never hand-write what the engine exports (a price loop, a buy-max, an
achievement check, a focus trap, a countdown), never copy engine source here, never edit `node_modules/watchpot`.
The test for anything new: strip this game's nouns from its names; if what is left still reads as a thing, it is
generic and goes to the engine, released as a tag, before it is used here.

