## The shader floor

`watchpot/shader` is the container; the look is this game's own GLSL, in `src/shaders/`, one file per surface, each
with a one-line comment saying what it is in the fiction. The scaffold's backdrop (`src/shaders/{{surface}}.ts`) is a
starter in the right direction, never the finished thing. A finished game has at least:

1. **A backdrop that is the place**: one `ShaderCanvas` behind the page, the fiction's room moving slowly.
2. **A hero surface**: the one thing the player looks at is a shader or sits in one, brightening with the numbers.
3. **Light as feedback**: at least one state the player cares about shows as light, not a colour swap.
4. **The moment**: a shader transition or a uniform driven to its extreme, not just a modal opening.

Every surface has a `still` (a CSS gradient of the same idea), holds a chosen frame under reduced motion, logs
`onError`, has a `maxPixels` budget, and reads its colours through `useShaderTokens`, never hex in GLSL. Drive them
from the same few 0..1 uniforms (`u_heat` from the income, `u_pulse` on an event, `u_progress`, `u_depth`); values
that move every frame go through the `ref` handle's `setUniforms`. Small surfaces share one `ShaderPool`. happy-dom
cannot compile GLSL: a shader counts only once it has drawn in the browser with a clean console.

## Map

- `src/content/` the data: words (the vocabulary), the economy, the core's list, and one file per feature;
  `pacing.ts` is the session schedule and the pacing contract
- `src/game/` the features on the engine's factories (`wallet`, `stats`, `settings`, the core and the chosen
  features; `stats.ts` is the engine's `stats`, fed by every feature's contributions), `income.ts`, `game.ts`
  (`defineGame`) and the store
- `src/app/` the page (`App.tsx` over `src/ui`'s `Shell`), the tabs, the header, the about sheet (the settings and
  the save as text), the icons, the sounds (zzfx only) · `src/screens/` one screen per tab and the main screen's parts
- `src/shaders/` the backdrop's fragment and its still
- `src/ui/` this game's own components: thin wrappers over the engine's headless `watchpot/ui`, each with its CSS
  module, and `theme.css` (the token contract and the page's base); `src/ui/README.md` lists every token
- `design/tokens.json` the palette and type, compiled to `src/tokens.css` by `bun run tokens` (commit both); the
  fonts are Google-hosted, linked from `index.html`
- `sim/` the headless sim and its timeline row · `test/` the game's tests

## Commands

- `bun run dev` serves the game at http://localhost:3000 (HMR off: refresh; `PORT=<n>` for another port).
- `bun run verify`: typecheck, lint, the tests the change touches, and `test/decimals.test.ts` (no two Decimals
  compared with an operator: use `.lt()`, `.gte()`, `.eq()`). Green before every commit.
- `bun run gates`: typecheck, lint, every fast test, the build. `bun run gates:full` adds the slow tier and the
  pacing contract.
- `bun run sim -- [--hours=4] [--seed=sim] [--contract]`: played hours on `activeSchedule`, the contract judged at
  its hours. One run for a ballpark, never a loop.
- `bun run sim -- --sessions [--days=7] [--seed=sim]`: the session schedule, days of coming and going, never judged.
- `#dev` on the dev server opens the engine's dev panel (time warp, each feature's state, the save).
- `bun run tokens` after editing `design/tokens.json`. `bun run lint:fix` formats.
- Never name a package script `check` (Bun 1.4.3+ typechecks with `bun check`).

## The engine

`watchpot` is installed by tag: `"watchpot": "git+ssh://github.com/backshelf/watchpot.git#{{engine}}"` (never
`github:` or `git+https://`, which 404 on the private repository). To move to a newer engine, change the tag and run
`bun install`. A change the engine needs is made in its own repository and released as a tag.

## Making a change

1. Fix it where the rule lives: numbers and words in `src/content/`, rules in the feature in `src/game/`, looks in
   the screen, the component's module or `design/tokens.json`.
2. A bug fix gets one test that fails on the old code. A new rule or mechanic gets a test only for what can break in
   it (an ordering, an invariant, a boundary). No test for content, copy, a retune, a look, or anything the sim or the
   type checker already pins: every test is paid for at every gate.
3. A change to a saved shape bumps `save.version` in `src/game/game.ts` with a migration step.
4. A tuning change runs the sim once with `--contract`; a band moved on purpose says why in the commit.
5. `bun run verify`, Conventional Commit on `main`, no trailers.

## Code rules

- Screens read the store only through `useGameState` and `useGameStore` (`src/app/hooks.ts`). A selector never
  returns a fresh object or Decimal (it re-renders forever): select the state and work it out in the render.
- The components in `src/ui` are this game's: restyle them in their own module. Place one from a screen through a
  wrapper or a custom property, never by passing a class that sets a property its module also sets (two CSS modules
  on one element resolve by bundle order). Their CSS reads colour, type and radius only from the `--ui-*` tokens.
- No English inside a `src/ui` component: words come from `src/content/` through props.
- In JSX, `key` comes before any spread props.
- `motion/react` owns `transform` and `opacity` on what it animates. Never name a CSS `container`. Biome refuses
  `!important`.
- One wall-clock read: `createGameStore`'s `now`. Game logic reads `api.now()` or the step's `ctx`.
- Sounds are cues in `src/app/sound.ts`: zzfx voices, or a pack's recordings as `takes` with the voice kept until
  the pack has loaded.
- `bun test` runs on happy-dom: no CSS, no layout. Assert elements by name. A test over a second is `*.slow.test.ts`.

## Git

Conventional Commits, imperative and lowercase (`type(scope): summary`). No `Co-authored-by` or other trailers.
Never force-push. Git Bash crash dumps (`bash.exe.stackdump`) are never staged.
